January. 2044. Oxfordshire.
Hannah had sworn never to marry again. Once was enough for an entire lifetime. A promise she’d made to herself. Resolute. She'd never tie herself to someone. The cost had been too great, the scar tissue of a life she’d once lived still painful. It came to her in fragments: moments in the mundane that exposed a wound long thought healed. Her couples therapy, the school run, standing in near tears in Waitrose, drained from work trying to figure out dinner. Sometimes, when stressed, Hannah dreamed of the picturesque red brick house just outside of Milton Keynes. Waking up heart thumping and drenched in sweat. Having to remind herself she wasn’t there anymore, stuck in the model middle-class life that she’d been told to covet since her girlhood. It was gone now. The home she’d bought both of her girls back to when still bleeding and high on exhaustion was now a Tesco supermarket. The village had been swallowed up by the urban sprawl like many before it.
In truth, she’d sworn she’d never marry again, unless one singular person asked. Her marriage had collapsed, tearing through the valley of her life without discrimination. Seeking to destroy everything she and her husband had spent almost two decades building. It had been worth it, once the calamity had stopped and the dust had settled. She wouldn’t take the risk unless it was worth the price. She’d spent eleven years in Maranello, part of the team vital in returning the ‘Prancing Horse’ to its former glory. Hannah had blossomed; her forties had once been a decade of terror. Told she would wither and rot as all women of a certain age had to. Instead, she became herself. No longer moored by the expectations of others. Unconstrained she’d grown upwards; secure, confident, unapologetically able to name her desires.
Hannah had been convinced back to England with an eye-watering sum from the group who now owned Williams to become their Head of Strategy, and two years before, to take the role as their Team Principal. Her eldest remained on the continent with her father, though following her mother's footsteps, working towards a PhD in Mechanical Engineering. Hannah's youngest daughter, a freer spirit than her big sister, was now finally in university a couple of years into studying medicine after spending a few years ‘finding herself’ backpacking around the globe. Hannah hadn’t minded. Well, she’d tried very hard not to mind. Steadfast in not becoming a copy of her mother and forcing her daughters into a life they could not bear living. She tried her best to smile through all the stories she’d hear when her daughter would come home on the weekends, a suitcase filled with textbooks she’d ignored so she was able to get nice snacks from M&S and use Hannah’s washing machine without sending up the electricity bill in her house share.
Returning to England had done nothing but start the cycle again. Almost twenty-five years of desire and denial. It started as it always had; completely innocent. Though how innocent could it have been if they’d played this game countless times before? The years spent switching between the one needing to fall and the other being the shoulder to rest against. It was reassuring in a way that no matter what happened, there was a person who knew the darkest stains of your character. Yet never think of turning you away. Laura had been certain they wouldn’t start yet again, but the time between them was too great to abandon Hannah completely. Both now in their fifties, she’d sworn blind they were far too old and far too clever to keep falling into bed with each other again. It had taken only two and a half months since Hannah’s return to England before they found themselves laid in bed beside the other in Laura’s home in Oxford. ‘We can’t do this again,’ they’d both swear before the magnetic pull became too strong to resist. No matter how many years had come since that night in the tail end of the Australian summer when Hannah had kissed a drunken Laura and begged her to go to bed, it still felt the same. Both with more grey hair and deeper lines etched on their faces, it felt so achingly familiar each time they reunited. The feeling of calm that washed over Hannah whenever Laura was back sleeping softly beside just as it had that very first night in twenty-six. 
These four years had been the longest they’d managed to go without a crisis; some terminal issue that would force them apart again. They both held their breath, sure something would shift and render them incompatible once more. As the months turned to years, they’d started to allow themselves to hope. Quietly at first, and in private. Both deeply ashamed of how much they longed to build a life together. Terrified that admitting it would urge the universe to take it from them once more. It had happened almost imperceptibly: a toothbrush left in a bathroom, a book left dog-eared on the bedside table, setting two places at the table. Two different lives crept together until you couldn’t see where one ended and the other began.
