‘Are you ready?’ Laura huffed, her silver-streaked hair still in large Velcro rollers.
‘Almost,’ Hannah said calmly, her focus still on the light brown eyeshadow she was dusting across her eyelid.
‘We should have got someone–’
‘You said we didn’t need a make-up artist. Or someone to do your hair–’
‘Well I'm stupid. The back of my head still doesn’t look right.’ Laura huffed. Hannah could see her hand shaking as she tried to put her earrings in. ‘The universe is letting me know I’m wrong.’
‘It’s just hair–’
‘It is not just hair!’ Laura’s voice was exasperated. ‘It’s my fucking wedding day! And my hair looks ridiculous.’
‘I think you look gorgeous,’ she soothed. A smile crept onto her face as she looked at Laura, the pale silver-blue silk of her dress draped over her wide hips, swinging around her calves. They’d both felt too long in the tooth to wear white, but Laura had an expensive dress she’d spent far too much on for George’s retirement party that felt almost bridal. ‘Would you like a hand?’ Hannah's voice was quiet.
‘No!’ Laura scowled. ‘I don’t need a mother.’
‘I would hope not.’ Hannah scoffed. ‘But I’d hope as your soon-to-be wife you’d let me help you with your bloody earring.’
‘I can manage.’ Laura snapped.
Hannah’s mouth tightened for a moment. She wasn’t drunk on mimosas and starving hungry too scared to eat before having to cram herself into a tulle monstrosity in case her stomach bloated, and she’d look porky in the wedding photos like the first time she got married. Perhaps having had the big white fantasy wedding before meant she didn’t feel the nerves now. She stood slowly, as if not to startle Laura. One hand grazing her hip, the other taking the earring from her hand and setting it down on the dresser carefully.
‘Laura,’ her voice was low.
‘Just leave it.’ Hannah felt her chest go tight when Laura’s voice cracked.
‘How can I leave it?’ she whispered. Her breath tickled the sensitive skin of Laura’s neck.
‘I’m fine –’
‘Well that’s a load of rubbish.’ Hannah laughed. Her thumb rubbed  Laura’s knuckle, trying to get her hand to stop trembling.
‘I mean it.’
‘You’re as terrible a liar as I am.’
Laura sighed deeply. Silence hung between them. Downstairs she could hear Hannah’s daughters talking with her niece, bickering about something she gratefully couldn’t quite hear. It was all unimportant now. She squeezed Hannah’s soft hand tightly; the smell her perfume wafting through the air. Even now it was the same as decades before. White florals from Jo Malone she’d buy religiously from the big Boots store in town whenever her bottle got low. She smelled the same as that night in São Paulo over twenty years before; scared and alone, Laura had clung to her. She’d hated Hannah for a while, resenting how expertly she’d been caught in her web. It hadn’t lasted, not properly. Laura had decades to make peace with it, having then had the pleasure of learning first-hand how cruel you can become when you’re unhappy. Hannah had taken the brunt of her failed engagement; the venue and honeymoon booked, the caterers paid for, and the dress that made her stomach churn every time she’d tried it on hanging in her wardrobe. It had been Hannah who’d spent hours on the phone listening to Laura as she tried to convince herself to go through with the wedding for months, and getting her head bitten off for the trouble every time she’d gently suggested Laura postpone until she was sure it was what she wanted. It had also been Hannah who she had run to when she’d finally called the whole thing off nine days before the date was set; her head laid in Hannah’s lap, hand stroking her hair as Laura sobbed like a child. Hannah had been called back to Italy at the end of summer, and they’d fallen from each other's lives yet again.
‘I’m scared.’ Laura admitted.
‘Me too.’ Hannah’s voice was barely above a whisper. She lifted Laura’s hand to her mouth, pressing a kiss against her skin, leaving a smear of pink gloss on her golden skin.
‘I feel like we’ve been so close so many times,’ she breathed.
'I know.'
‘If it goes wrong–’
‘It won’t –’
‘How the fuck can you be certain it won't then?’
‘I’m not, but–’
Laura pulled away, starting to pace along across the room, her hand fidgeting with the rings on her finger. ‘Then why are we doing this? If you’re not certain?’ Laura stopped pacing, her gaze focused on Hannah much like when she’d scowl at Kenzo when he’d ignore her instructions on the radio. ‘Things are good between us… so good… what if we mess it up?’
‘We won’t–’
‘But you said you weren’t certain!’ Laura shrieked.
‘Jesus! We can’t be certain of anything. Maybe this could all go wrong, and we mess it up. Or we could finally be bloody happy!’
‘But what if–’
‘What if it goes well, Laura?’ Hannah pushed, closing the gap between them. Her sweet grey eyes locked on hers. ‘What if we focus on the now, instead of giving up all hope because something might happen?’
‘But something always happens.’ A twinkling tear rolled down Laura’s cheek. ‘I’m too old to start again if…’ Her voice faded out.
‘I wasted so many years being scared. I don’t want to waste more on the off-chance something could happen.’ Hannah breathed, slipping her hand to Laura’s waist. ‘And I don’t want to live with the regret that we didn’t try properly. If it ends and we’ve given it our best go, then I could handle that. But it would kill me to know we could bring each other so much joy and not at least try.’ ​
‘I don’t want to lose you again’, Laura murmured. She’d spent so long being second to everything in Hannah’s life: her career, her children, her husband. Even when Hannah was single, there had been the insurmountable difference of distance and kids. When Hannah had finally returned to England to take the lead at Williams, she’d sworn to herself not to let it start again. She'd been here before countless times; a few stolen weeks tangled up in hotel rooms dreaming of a future together before the real world reminded them it could never be more than a fantasy. With bated breath, Laura spent the past four years waiting for the inevitable demise. It hadn’t come yet, and with every night spent with Hannah’s warm body beside her, she grew more fearful of the day it would. Had it all been a mistake? 
The second happiest day of her life after standing on the podium for the first time in Silverstone with George had been on a quiet white sand beach in Eastern Tasmania last summer. She’d sat with Hannah looking out across the deep turquoise water, heart thumping as she fumbled to pull the navy velvet box from her pocket. Laura had sworn blind she never wanted to marry; perhaps it was age and wanting security as she grew older. Or the terror of losing Hannah to the tides of their lives yet again. But Laura hated how much she wanted it now.‘How about it then?’ Had hardly been the romantic declaration of undying love she’d imagined countless times in her head; her brain blanking as Hannah’s sun-pink face looked at her in shock. Gratefully, Hannah had put her out of her misery quickly, squealing excitedly like a schoolgirl and kissing her happily. The memory of Hannah sprawled out in the hotel bed wearing nothing but the modest diamond ring twinkling on her finger still took Laura’s breath away.
‘You won’t lose me.’ Hannah whispered, her delicate finger brushing away another year that had rolled down Laura’s cheek. ‘No matter how far I’ve gone, I’ve always come back.’
‘I wish I’d asked you to stay… Not let you go back to fucking Italy.’
‘You said you didn’t want me to get you a job in Ferrari.’ Hannah counted gently. ‘And you certainly didn’t want a life with children.’
‘But I don’t want you to go again.’ Laura felt pathetic, a woman of almost fifty-three crying like a small child. ‘Every time things look right, something makes you leave me alone again.’
​‘I won’t,’ Hannah pressed. ‘Besides, I’m stuck here.’ She teased quietly, a half-smile on her thin mouth. ‘I can’t afford to get out of my contract even if I wanted to.’
‘Stop trying to be funny.’ Laura sniffled. ​
‘I know, that’s your job. But I can try though.’ Hannah’s hand left Laura’s waist. She moved upwards to cradle her face. ‘Whatever happens. We’ll face it, you and me.’ Hannah gravelled.
‘I love you.’ Laura croaked. It made Hannah’s heart thump every time. Laura was German - She never said it often; in fact, she didn’t say it for months after Hannah said it first, and it had been torture. But Hannah knew, every time Laura would lay her head on her chest after a long day, or leave a mug of tea and toast slathered with marmalade next to the bed so Hannah could steal a few more minutes asleep, that Laura loved her.
‘I love you too.’ Laura leaned inward, pressing her mouth to Hannah’s. They’d both have to redo their make-up, but that was the least of her concerns. Hannah felt Laura shaking as she clung to her; she wished she could swallow up all of her fear. Hannah was used to carrying it; she was able to handle it. She wanted to spend her life making Laura feel nothing but joy after the years of torment loving her had caused. ​‘I mean it.’ Hannah whispered. ‘Whatever happens, I’m not going anywhere.’ Her hand reached to the dresser for the tasteful bezel-set diamond earrings Hannah had gotten Laura for her fiftieth birthday. Steady hands got the hook through the hole in her earlobe with little struggle. ‘Easy.’ Her voice was smug.
