The day had been understated. Family and a few selected close friends to celebrate the day with them: Hannah’s sister and her family. Laura’s parents and brother too. Hannah’s mother had quietly arrived, the rift between them better in the years passed but not completely healed. Laura was grateful she was there, knowing the nights Hannah had spent fretting about whether to invite her, and then whether or not she would even come.
Hannah’s daughters had handed her a bouquet, telling her she just had to have one for her big day.  She’d been grateful that the two girls with kind grey eyes just like their mothers had accepted her so warmly. Even if Laura still felt unsteady in her relationship with them, clouded undoubtedly by the guilt of being the catalyst in their parents’ divorce. But as they’d grown into young women, Laura had felt more at ease when they’d call her, inevitably for a problem they felt too ashamed to turn to Hannah about, and ask for her assistance. She cared for them both deeply, no matter how awkward being called their ‘not- stepmother’ was.
In classic English fashion, the sky had never turned from a moody grey throughout the whole day. Cold sleet had fallen over Oxford, and the wind had displaced Laura’s hair the second her modest heel had hit the pavement outside the town hall. She hadn’t cared for one solitary moment; even when the pictures has turned out badly, she’d not been able to see anything beyond how Hannah looked at her. Eyes heavy with devotion as they swore before whatever god they both didn’t believe in to spend the rest of their lives together. Seven-thousand, seven-hundred and twenty-six days between their first kiss in a cheap hotel room across the world and the kiss that sealed their union a few miles from the home they’d tentatively built together.
Dinner had been a quiet affair in a restaurant twenty minutes outside in Oxford, in Minister Lovell; in between the city they lived in, and the small hotel in the Cotswolds they had two precious nights as newlyweds tucked away together before returning to the pre-season grind. Hannah’s hand held hers, or lay reassuringly on her knee throughout the evening. Most of her panic from the morning was forgotten in her happiness. Though buried deep inside her chest, doubt lingered; happy endings were for children, or the simple-minded. Things had been so gloriously easy these past few years it felt like a trap. That her happiness was so sure to be snatched from her. Laura decided she’d try not to worry tonight, instead make use of the large claw-foot bath in the quiet hotel with her beautiful wife.
