Light crept through the crack in the curtains, irritating Hannah’s eyes before the buzz of her alarm clock could set her nerves on edge. Set a precious fifteen minutes that it should be. It always was when she wasn’t alone.
‘Fuck,’ Laura gravelled. Her voice hoarse from sleep and the dry air-conditioning. Hannah liked it best when Laura was sleepy; her usually blow-out mane all mussed, the usually bright face soft with fatigue. All of the bravado she wore when around her colleagues was taken off and left with her clothes at the door of whatever nondescript hotel room they found a few stolen moments together in. This time, Hannah’s. Her team principal role meant she usually got a nicer one. Laura swore every time she wouldn’t stay - wanting the extra minutes in bed to help salve the tiredness that had settled into her bones. The final of two triple-header weekends that had finished off the first half of the season before summer break had taken its toll. Hannah had tried to convince her to put her laptop away when their room service had been delivered, to no avail. It had been a small miracle she hadn’t tried checking the telemetry data one last time when Hannah’s mouth had been between her legs.
‘You have to get up,’ Hannah cooed, her hand gliding down the warm skin of Laura’s long back.
‘No, I don’t.’ Laura grumbled, tugging the duvet up over her head to blot out the light.
‘The longer you wait, the worse it will be.’
‘No. I’m good here.’ Her muffled voice came from beneath the duvet.
Hannah understood why Laura was hesitant to leave; their relationship wasn’t the foul secret it had once been. But it wasn’t public; both were private people, even more so with the conservative views of the industry they found themselves in. There was little they both anted less than to be the subject of gossip. In private they could be the McMillan-Müllers, but here they were just two people. Often photographed entering the paddock together, or sitting outside one of the team motorhomes drinking coffee. It wasn't as if they were trading blows for wins anymore either. Laura was now head of race engineering at Mercedes and Hannah was leading the midfield with Williams. A role reversal of their Red Bull-Haas days that still made Hannah chuckle. She wanted nothing more than to spend the day warm and safe, snuggled up in a luxurious hotel room with Laura. Far more than desperately trying to keep the team motivated to start getting points consistently anyway. She’d gotten more than a few new twinkling silver hairs this season as the reliability issues from the quick-yet-fragile Ford engine she’d chosen to go for last season instead of the Mercedes ones of countless years before had significant teething issues.
‘Laura.’ Hannah sighed. Her long-retired Mum-voice engaged once more, and she hated herself for it. But she had to get Laura up and out before the mechanics got up really to fight each other for the pick of the hash browns over breakfast. The duvet peeled back, and Laura is scowling. Hannah would cross, but Laura looked far too precious, pouting like a displeased child to do anything more than laugh.
‘Don’t laugh at me!’ Laura groaned, burying her face into the pillow.
‘Then stop acting like a teenager.’
‘Hannah. Five more minutes.’ She bargained.
‘I did try to get you to go to bed before one in the morning.’’
Laura turned her head. A wry smile on her full mouth. ‘You weren’t complaining that much.
A flush of embarrassment bled across her pale face. ‘I’m getting up.’ Hannah says, mouth tight.
‘Noooo,’ Laura groans, her hand closing around Hannah’s delicate wrist.
‘One more weekend,’ Hannah purred. ‘And then we’re on honeymoon, and we can do nothing but sleep in for two whole weeks… But I need to get ready.’ Hannah sighed. The joy of their long-delayed honeymoon bled away as the thought of qualifying crept back into her head. Would the engines hold? Would an ageing Franco in his final season keep it out of the wall today? How many questions that niggled at her doubt would try and undo her?
‘Stop spiralling.’ Laura didn’t even have her eyes open to know what Hannah was thinking, or the fear marked clear on her face.  
‘I’m not,’ she blustered, stepping from the warmth of their bed and padding to the bathroom.
‘Bullshit.’ She could hear Laura roll over in bed as she turned the shower on, waiting a few seconds before she stepped under the warm water. ‘You totally are.’ Laura’s voice made her jump, having not heard her wife’s footsteps as she joined her under the water.
‘Well, I quite like having a job,’ Hannah said, rolling her eyes as Laura passed her the shampoo with practised familiarity.
‘I like you having a job too,’ Laura grinned, a stupid smile on her tired face. ‘You got weird during your gardening leave last time.’
‘I did not,’ Hannah snapped, angling her head under the water to rinse away the suds.
‘You made thirty-eight loaves of Banana bread. No one in our family even likes banana bread.’ Laura scoffed, handing her the conditioner.
‘I was trying to figure out why exactly the first three fail–’ Her voice cut out. Trying to figure out the issues on some undoubtedly AI-generated recipe she’d seen on Instagram so obsessively had definitely been weird. The house was quieter than the last time she'd been on gardening leave; both girls were still at home then, and Hannah was trying to juggle the fallout of her divorce and the subsequent custody battle that had followed, all while getting ready to move to Italy. Her focus turned to her conditioner, embarrassment flooding her body and turning her face scarlet. The small cubicle echoed with Laura’s triumphant laughter.
‘I won't say I told you so–’
‘Then don't.’ Hannah scowled, trying hard not to break as her wife grinned like an idiot.
‘But it's so fun for me.’
‘Well, as long as you're having fun then.’ Hannah grumbled, massaging the conditioner into the lengths of her greying hair. ‘If you're not washing your hair, you need to go get dressed.’ Hannah ordered.
‘I’ll eat when I'm at the track.’ Laura shrugged, rinsing the lathered soap from her skin. ‘I won't get time with you before tomorrow night.’
‘You’re a big girl. I'm sure you'll manage a few hours without mocking me.’
‘I’m not mocking you.’ Laura feigned upset at the accusation.
‘Pfft. You totally are.’ Hannah chided. ‘And stop pouting the –’
‘Wind will change, and I'll be stuck looking miserable forever.’ Laura finished. Having heard it countless times before over the past twenty-one years.
Hannah stepped from the shower, leaving Laura under the drumming water. A plush hotel towel wrapped around her body as she focused on getting ready for the day. Hannah wiped the steam from the mirror with her free hand as she scrubbed her teeth, pausing a moment to poke at the lines around her eyes she hated so viscerally. She felt even more tired than she looked; the stress of running a midfield team was even greater than the pressure of decades spent fighting at the front.​
her eyes lingered, watching on as Laura stepped from the shower, water dripping from her bare skin. Even now she was still the most beautiful person Hannah had ever laid eyes on. Her hips a little wider, stomach slightly softer after the menopause, and gravity acting on her once high set breasts. None of it mattered. Not one single bit. She studied her wife in awe as Laura swaggered towards her, leaning forward to press a soft kiss against her shoulder.
‘Try not to worry too much.’ Laura breathed, voice quiet and sincere. ‘I still think you made the right choice with Ford.’
‘I think I did too.’ She sighed, bending forward to spit her toothpaste. ‘I’m just tired of getting ripped apart every time we have another DNF –’
‘Franco put himself in the wall yesterday. Not the bloody engine.’ Laura consoled. ‘He’s a WDC. He should know better.’
‘He’s gone this year. Deciding to focus on his daughter going into F3 instead.’
‘I’d heard he was retiring to own a football team.’
‘No. I mean I think he owns one. But no, all Agus…I think.’​
